{
  "dom_id": "step-q115661",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+16}{(x-7)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+16}{(x-7)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-7}+\\frac{B}{(x-7)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-7)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-7)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9x+16=A(x-7)^{2}+B(x-7)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=7,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(7)^{2}-9(7)+16 & =A(7-7)^{2}+B(7-7)+C \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=6$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6)^{2}-9(6)+16 & =A(6-7)^{2}+B(6-7)+C \\\\ -2 & =A-B+2 \\\\ A-B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=8$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8)^{2}-9(8)+16 & =A(8-7)^{2}+B(8-7)+C \\\\ 8 & =A+B+2 \\\\ A+B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=-4$ and $A+B=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =-4+6 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =6 \\\\ B & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+16}{(x-7)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x-7}+\\frac{5}{(x-7)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-7)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+16}{(x-7)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-7}+\\frac{5}{(x-7)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x-7)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-7}\\,\\text{d}x+5\\int \\frac{1}{(x-7)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{(x-7)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-7|-\\frac{5}{x-7}-\\frac{1}{(x-7)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant.",
